<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. du Pré, 28, comes for her yearly preventive visit five months after starting ocrelizumab. She has had no relapse; the cystitis in June cleared with nitrofurantoin and the tingling that came with it went within two days. She practices three to four hours a day, played chamber music in public last month for the first time this year, and teaches two students a week. Fatigue comes in the late afternoon and she plans her day around it. She takes cholecalciferol 4000 units daily. Her PHQ-9 today is 3. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Periodic adult preventive visit, age 28</td></tr><tr><td>Active conditions</td><td>Relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis on ocrelizumab; vitamin D deficiency on replacement</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Ocrelizumab every six months (next dose next month); cholecalciferol 4000 units daily</td></tr><tr><td>Mood</td><td>PHQ-9 3</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed by the patient on a tablet in the waiting room and reviewed with her by Dr. Thackeray-Osei. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Good, and much better than in the spring</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Busy with practice, teaching and concerts; worries about another relapse; good support from friends</td></tr><tr><td>Tobacco</td><td>Never smoked</td></tr><tr><td>Alcohol and drugs</td><td>A glass of wine two or three evenings a week; no drugs</td></tr><tr><td>Physical activity</td><td>Swims twice a week and walks daily, as the occupational therapist suggested for fatigue</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Regular meals; no restrictions</td></tr><tr><td>Sleep</td><td>Eight hours; rests for half an hour after lunch</td></tr><tr><td>Sexual and reproductive health</td><td>Condoms used consistently; not planning a pregnancy this year</td></tr><tr><td>Safety at home</td><td>Feels safe; no one hurts, threatens or controls her</td></tr><tr><td>Road safety</td><td>Wears a seat belt; does not drive when tired</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

